# Running heavy jobs on USC CARC

The user has access to USC CARC (Discovery cluster, Slurm). Do not launch long or
GPU-heavy jobs locally (full training runs, multi-seed sweeps, large evaluations).
Instead, tell the user the job is ready and give them the exact commands to run on CARC;
they will run it and paste back the output.

- Fine to run locally: unit/smoke tests, `data.frac=0.02 train.epochs=1` debug runs, quick scripts (< ~5 min).
- Everything else: hand off to CARC.

When handing off, always provide:
1. Any code to sync first (files changed since the last run).
2. The exact `sbatch` command, e.g.
   `sbatch --array=0-2 carc/train.slurm configs/phase1_baseline.yaml`
3. Estimated runtime and what to paste back: the Slurm log in `logs/` and
   `outputs/<experiment>/seed<N>/metrics.json`.

CARC conventions used in this repo:
- One-time setup: `bash carc/setup.sh` (conda env `csci566`, dataset in `/scratch1/$USER/data`).
- Job script: `carc/train.slurm <config> [overrides...]`; seeds come from the Slurm array index.
- Edit `#SBATCH --account` in `carc/train.slurm` if the project ID changes (`myaccount` shows it).
